    "long_instructions": "# Scenario\r\n\r\nA 4th grade science teacher is teaching a unit on weather and Earth systems. In this lesson, students are learning about the water cycle.\r\n\r\nThe lesson objectives are for students to:\r\n Identify and describe the main stages of the water cycle—evaporation, condensation, and precipitation\r\n Explain the cycle using a simple, real-world example.\r\n\r\nTo support a short mini-lesson, the teacher decides to use an AI tool to generate a student-friendly explanation of the water cycle.\r\n\r\nThe teacher enters the following prompt:\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"width:80%; margin:0 auto 18px auto;\">\r\n  <div style=\"background:#F7F8FA; border-left:5px solid #8C52BA; border-radius:14px; padding:18px;\">\r\n    <div style=\"font-size:16px;\">\r\n      ✨ Explain the water cycle.\r\n    </div>\r\n  </div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\nThe AI generates this response:\r\n\r\n<div style=\"width:80%; background:#F7F8FA; border-left:5px solid #000000; border-radius:14px; padding:14px; margin:0 auto 18px auto;\"\">\r\n<div style=\"font-size:16px\">\r\nThe water cycle, also known as the hydrologic cycle, is a continuous process by which water circulates through the Earth's atmosphere, lithosphere, and hydrosphere. It involves several key processes, including evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and infiltration. During evaporation, water transforms from a liquid to a vapor due to solar energy. Transpiration refers to the release of water vapor from plants. This vapor condenses into clouds, eventually leading to precipitation in the form of rain, snow, sleet, or hail. Water then infiltrates the ground or collects in bodies of water, continuing the cycle.\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## Reflection\r\n\r\n> In the reflection box below, answer the following questions:\r\n> <br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp 1. How successful was the teacher’s prompt in producing a useful explanation for 4th grade students?\r\n> <br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp 2. Write a revised version of the prompt. Include what specific changes you made and why.\r\n\r\nTake this activity further by trying out your new prompt in an LLM of your choice.",
